fills:
It is the clay that makes the earth stick to his spade;
He fills in holes like this year after year;
The others have gone; they were tired, and half afraid,

filaments:
In some firm fabric, woven in and out;
Your golden filaments in fair design
Across my duller fibre.  And to-day

fille:
scented hair,
Une jolie fille à vendre, très cher;
A thing of gaiety, a thing of sorrow,

files:
I have elected one.
When sense from spirit files away,
And subterfuge is done;

filched:
A child’s too costly for so mere a wretch;
She filched it somewhere; and it means, with her,
Instead of honour, blessing, ... merely shame.

‘Now, look to it, coves, that all the beef and drink
Be not filched from us like the other fun;
For beer’s spilt easier than a woman is!

fillets:
Of languid and sequestered ease;
Her girdles and her fillets gleam
Like changing fires on sunset seas;

filigree:
On an ivory fruit;
And a filigree frost
Of frail notes lost

From his candle's tulip-flame.
He set the filigree hands; he laid
The watch in the case which he had made;
